L.push({id:"g4", unit:"F4", title:"Grape Families, Synonyms and Crossings", mins:10,
summary:"One grape, six names: the synonyms every exam asks, the family tree behind the classics, and how crossings differ from hybrids.",
body:`
<p>Wine lists and exam papers use local names. A guest who loves Shiraz may not know she also loves Syrah; a candidate who doesn't know that Tinta Roriz, Tinto Fino and Aragonez are all Tempranillo will lose easy marks at every level. Learn the names in families.</p>
<h3>The synonyms that are tested most</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Grape</th><th>Also called</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tempranillo</td><td>Tinto Fino, Tinta del País (Ribera del Duero); Tinta de Toro (Toro); Cencibel (La Mancha); Ull de Llebre (Catalonia); Tinta Roriz (Douro); Aragonez (Alentejo)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Grenache</td><td>Garnacha (Spain); Cannonau (Sardinia)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mourvèdre</td><td>Monastrell (Spain); Mataro (Australia, California)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Syrah</td><td>Shiraz (Australia, South Africa)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carignan</td><td>Cariñena, Mazuelo (Rioja), Samsó (Catalonia); Carignano (Sardinia)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Malbec</td><td>Côt (Loire, Cahors); Auxerrois (Cahors)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Zinfandel</td><td>Primitivo (Puglia); Tribidrag, Crljenak Kaštelanski (Croatia, its home)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sangiovese</td><td>Brunello (Montalcino); Prugnolo Gentile (Montepulciano); Morellino (Scansano); Nielluccio (Corsica)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nebbiolo</td><td>Spanna (Alto Piemonte); Chiavennasca (Valtellina); Picotendro (Valle d'Aosta)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Noir</td><td>Spätburgunder (Germany); Blauburgunder (Austria, Switzerland); Pinot Nero (Italy)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Gris</td><td>Pinot Grigio (Italy); Grauburgunder, Ruländer (Germany)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pinot Blanc</td><td>Weissburgunder (Germany, Austria); Pinot Bianco (Italy)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chenin Blanc</td><td>Steen (South Africa); Pineau de la Loire</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trebbiano Toscano</td><td>Ugni Blanc (Cognac, Armagnac)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vermentino</td><td>Rolle (Provence); Pigato (Liguria); Favorita (Piedmont)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Macabeo</td><td>Viura (Rioja)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains</td><td>Moscato Bianco (Asti); Muskateller; Moscatel de Grano Menudo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscat of Alexandria</td><td>Zibibbo (Sicily, Pantelleria); Moscatel de Setúbal; Hanepoot (South Africa)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blaufränkisch</td><td>Lemberger (Germany, Washington); Kékfrankos (Hungary)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Savagnin</td><td>Traminer; Heida or Païen (Valais)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Welschriesling</td><td>Graševina (Croatia); Olaszrizling (Hungary). Not related to Riesling.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mencía</td><td>Jaen (Dão)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Albariño</td><td>Alvarinho (Vinho Verde)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Godello</td><td>Gouveio (Douro)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durif</td><td>Petite Sirah (California)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Listán Prieto</td><td>País (Chile); Mission (California); Criolla Chica (Argentina)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Douce Noir</td><td>Bonarda (Argentina); Charbono (California)</td></tr></table>
<p>Three traps. <b>Melon de Bourgogne</b> is the grape of Muscadet, which is a place. <b>Verdejo</b> (Rueda) and <b>Verdelho</b> (Madeira, Australia) are different grapes. Chile's old "Sauvignon" was often <b>Sauvignonasse</b> (Friulano), and its "Merlot" was often <b>Carmenère</b>, identified only in 1994.</p>
<h3>The family tree</h3>
<ul><li><b>Pinot</b> is one very old variety. Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Pinot Blanc and Meunier are color and leaf <b>mutations</b> of it, not separate grapes.</li>
<li><b>Pinot × Gouais Blanc</b> (a humble peasant grape) produced Chardonnay, Gamay, Aligoté, Melon de Bourgogne and Auxerrois. Gouais is also a parent of Riesling.</li>
<li><b>Savagnin</b> (Traminer) is a parent of Sauvignon Blanc, Chenin Blanc, Grüner Veltliner and Silvaner.</li>
<li><b>Cabernet Franc</b> fathered the Bordeaux family: with Sauvignon Blanc it gave <b>Cabernet Sauvignon</b> (shown by DNA work at UC Davis in 1996); with Magdeleine Noire des Charentes it gave <b>Merlot</b>; with Gros Cabernet it gave <b>Carmenère</b>.</li>
<li><b>Syrah</b> is Dureza × Mondeuse Blanche, both from southeastern France: not from Shiraz in Persia.</li></ul>
<h3>Mutation, clone, crossing, hybrid</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td>Mutation</td><td>A spontaneous change within one variety</td><td>Pinot Gris from Pinot Noir; Gewürztraminer from Savagnin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clone</td><td>Vines propagated from one selected mother vine</td><td>Dijon clones 115, 667, 777 of Pinot Noir</td></tr>
<tr><td>Crossing</td><td>Two <i>Vitis vinifera</i> parents</td><td>Müller-Thurgau (Riesling × Madeleine Royale, 1882); Pinotage (Pinot Noir × Cinsault, 1925); Zweigelt (Blaufränkisch × St. Laurent, 1922); Scheurebe; Kerner; Marselan (Cabernet Sauvignon × Grenache, 1961)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hybrid</td><td>Vinifera crossed with another <i>Vitis</i> species</td><td>Vidal, Seyval Blanc, Baco Noir, Chambourcin; the disease-resistant PIWIs such as Regent, Solaris, Souvignier Gris and Voltis</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Teinturiers</b> are the rare grapes with red flesh as well as red skin: Alicante Bouschet, Saperavi, Dunkelfelder. They make deeply colored wine with no long maceration.</p>`,
floor:`<p>Translate for guests: "If you like Australian Shiraz, this Northern Rhône Syrah is the same grape in a cooler, more savory style." The Certified matching section pairs synonyms with regions; drill them in the Matching tab.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET uses the local name in its regional chapters (Tinta Roriz in the Douro, Spätburgunder in Germany). Use the name that fits the region you are writing about, and give the international name once in brackets.</p>`,
terms:[["Synonym","A different local name for the same grape variety."],["Mutation","A spontaneous genetic change within a variety, such as Pinot Gris from Pinot Noir."],["Crossing","A new variety bred from two Vitis vinifera parents."],["Teinturier","A grape with red flesh as well as red skin."],["Gouais Blanc","Old peasant grape; parent, with Pinot, of Chardonnay, Gamay and Aligoté."],["Tinta Roriz","Tempranillo in the Douro."],["Steen","Chenin Blanc in South Africa."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Tinta Roriz, Tinto Fino and Cencibel are all names for:","Tempranillo",["Touriga Nacional","Garnacha","Mencía"],"Tinta Roriz in the Douro, Tinto Fino in Ribera del Duero, Cencibel in La Mancha."],
[1,"Monastrell and Mataro are synonyms for:","Mourvèdre",["Carignan","Grenache","Cinsault"],"Monastrell in Spain, Mataro in Australia and California."],
[2,"Chiavennasca is the Valtellina name for:","Nebbiolo",["Barbera","Sangiovese","Dolcetto"],"It is Spanna in Alto Piemonte and Picotendro in the Valle d'Aosta."],
[2,"Which two grapes are the parents of Cabernet Sauvignon?","Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon Blanc",["Merlot and Cabernet Franc","Carmenère and Sémillon","Pinot Noir and Gouais Blanc"],"Shown by DNA analysis at UC Davis in 1996."],
[2,"Pinot Gris is best described as:","A color mutation of Pinot Noir",["A crossing of Pinot Noir and Riesling","A hybrid bred for cold","A clone of Chardonnay"],"Pinot Noir, Gris, Blanc and Meunier are forms of one variety."],
[2,"Müller-Thurgau is a crossing of Riesling and:","Madeleine Royale",["Silvaner","Gewürztraminer","Chasselas"],"Bred by Hermann Müller in 1882. It was long believed to be Riesling × Silvaner until DNA testing."],
[2,"A hybrid differs from a crossing because it:","Has a parent from another Vitis species",["Has two vinifera parents","Is a mutation of one variety","Is grown from a single mother vine"],"Vidal and Seyval Blanc are hybrids; Müller-Thurgau and Pinotage are crossings."],
[3,"Zinfandel is genetically identical to Puglia's Primitivo and Croatia's:","Tribidrag",["Plavac Mali","Pošip","Teran"],"Also called Crljenak Kaštelanski. Plavac Mali is one of its offspring."],
[3,"Which grape is a teinturier?","Alicante Bouschet",["Aglianico","Petit Verdot","Tannat"],"It has red flesh. Saperavi and Dunkelfelder are others."],
[2,"Steen is the traditional South African name for:","Chenin Blanc",["Sémillon","Colombard","Sauvignon Blanc"],"Chenin Blanc is South Africa's most planted grape."]
]});

L.push({id:"f14", unit:"F3", title:"Rosé in Depth", mins:8,
summary:"Four ways to make pink wine, what sets its color, the classic styles from Provence to Tavel to Cerasuolo, and how to sell it.",
body:`
<p>Rosé is no longer the wine trade's afterthought. Provence built a global luxury category on it in fifteen years, and every level of every exam now asks how it is made. The answer is always the same idea, applied for different lengths of time: <b>brief contact between clear juice and black grape skins</b>.</p>
<h3>Four methods</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Style and home</th></tr>
<tr><td>Direct pressing</td><td>Black grapes are pressed straight away, as for white wine; color comes only from the press cycle</td><td>The palest, most delicate rosés: most Côtes de Provence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short maceration</td><td>Crushed grapes soak on skins for a few hours up to a day or two, then the juice is run off and fermented cool</td><td>Deeper color and more body: Tavel, Bandol, Navarra, Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo</td></tr>
<tr><td>Saignée ("bleeding")</td><td>Juice is drawn from a tank of red must early in maceration. The rosé is a by-product; the red left behind is more concentrated</td><td>Often darker, riper and fuller, since the grapes were picked for red wine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blending</td><td>A little red wine is added to white</td><td>Normal for rosé Champagne. Not allowed for still PDO rosé in most of Europe; common in the New World</td></tr></table>
<h3>What sets the color</h3>
<p>Grape (Grenache and Cinsault are pale; Syrah and Mourvèdre deeper), time on skins, temperature, sulfur dioxide and pH. Pale is a fashion, not a mark of quality: many serious rosés are deeply colored. Winemakers protect fruit by picking at night, chilling the grapes, working under inert gas, fermenting cold in stainless steel and usually blocking malolactic conversion. Yeast choice matters: grapefruit and peach aromas in modern Provence rosé come largely from thiols and esters formed in a cold ferment.</p>
<h3>The styles to know</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Wine</th><th>Grapes</th><th>Style</th></tr>
<tr><td>Côtes de Provence</td><td>Grenache, Cinsault, Syrah, Mourvèdre, Tibouren, often with white Rolle</td><td>Very pale, dry, light, citrus and red berry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bandol rosé</td><td>Mourvèdre-led</td><td>Structured, savory, one of the few rosés that ages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tavel</td><td>Grenache-based</td><td>France's rosé-only appellation (1936): deep pink, dry, full</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rosé d'Anjou, Cabernet d'Anjou</td><td>Grolleau; Cabernet Franc and Sauvignon</td><td>Off-dry to medium, light</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sancerre and Marsannay rosé</td><td>Pinot Noir</td><td>Pale, taut, dry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rosé des Riceys</td><td>Pinot Noir</td><td>A rare still rosé from Champagne's Aube</td></tr>
<tr><td>Navarra and Rioja rosado</td><td>Garnacha, Tempranillo</td><td>Dry, fruity, strawberry; Cigales makes the traditional paler-red <b>clarete</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo</td><td>Montepulciano</td><td>Cherry-red, dry, almost a light red</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chiaretto di Bardolino</td><td>Corvina, Rondinella</td><td>Pale, salty, from Lake Garda</td></tr>
<tr><td>White Zinfandel</td><td>Zinfandel</td><td>Off-dry "blush," born from a stuck fermentation at Sutter Home in 1975</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weissherbst, Schilcher, Œil de Perdrix</td><td>One variety (Germany); Blauer Wildbacher (Austria); Pinot Noir (Neuchâtel)</td><td>Regional specialties worth recognizing on a label</td></tr></table>
<h3>The business</h3>
<p>France makes and drinks more rosé than any other country, and Provence is about 89% rosé. Château d'Esclans launched <b>Whispering Angel</b> with the 2006 vintage and proved that pink wine could be a premium brand; celebrity estates and large groups followed. The United States is Provence's largest export market. Most rosé is sold and drunk within a year or two of harvest, so vintage on the list matters: last year's is right, three years old usually is not, except for Bandol, Tavel and oak-aged examples.</p>
<h3>Service</h3>
<p>Serve at about 45-55°F in a white-wine glass. Rosé is the most forgiving food wine on a list: it has the acidity of a white and a touch of red-fruit weight, so it handles salads, grilled fish, charcuterie, garlic, tomatoes and spice. Offer it when a table is split between white and red.</p>`,
floor:`<p>Three questions sell rosé: pale and crisp, or fuller and fruitier? Dry, or a touch of sweetness? For the terrace or for dinner? Then one bottle: Provence for the first, Tavel or Bandol for the table that wants food wine.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>A model three-mark answer: "Short maceration: black grapes are crushed and left on skins for a few hours (1), extracting some colour and flavour but little tannin (1), then the juice is drained and fermented cool like a white wine (1)." Know why saignée also changes the red wine.</p>`,
terms:[["Direct pressing","Making rosé by pressing black grapes immediately, with almost no maceration."],["Saignée","Bleeding juice off a red-wine tank to make rosé and concentrate the red."],["Clarete","Traditional Spanish pale red or deep rosé, as in Cigales."],["Tavel","Southern Rhône appellation that makes only rosé."],["Rosé des Riceys","Still Pinot Noir rosé from the Aube, in Champagne."],["Weissherbst","German rosé made from a single grape variety."],["Blush","American term for pale, usually off-dry rosé such as White Zinfandel."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Which method gives the palest rosé?","Direct pressing",["Saignée","Two-day maceration","Carbonic maceration"],"Grapes are pressed at once, so the juice has only minutes of skin contact."],
[2,"In the saignée method, the rosé is:","Juice bled from a tank of red must",["A blend of red and white wine","Pressed from whole bunches","Made from red-fleshed grapes"],"Bleeding off juice also concentrates the red wine left in the tank."],
[2,"Grapefruit and peach aromas in modern Provence rosé come largely from:","A cold, protected ferment",["New oak barrels","Malolactic conversion","Long skin maceration"],"Thiols and esters form in a cold ferment under inert gas; oak and malolactic conversion are usually avoided."],
[2,"Which of these rosés is typically off-dry?","Cabernet d'Anjou",["Tavel","Bandol rosé","Sancerre rosé"],"Cabernet d'Anjou and Rosé d'Anjou keep some sweetness; Tavel, Bandol and Sancerre rosé are dry."],
[2,"Bandol rosé is built on which grape?","Mourvèdre",["Cinsault","Gamay","Pinot Noir"],"Mourvèdre gives Bandol rosé its structure and ability to age."],
[2,"Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is made from:","Montepulciano",["Sangiovese","Negroamaro","Corvina"],"Deep cherry in color; almost a light red."],
[3,"White Zinfandel began in 1975 with:","A stuck fermentation at Sutter Home",["A frost in Lodi","A blending error at Gallo","A Provence winemaker in Napa"],"The ferment stopped with sugar left, and the sweet pink wine sold out."],
[2,"A pale color in rosé indicates:","A stylistic choice, not quality",["A wine of higher quality","A wine lower in alcohol","Some residual sugar"],"Color follows grape and skin contact. Tavel and Cerasuolo are deep and serious."],
[2,"Rosé des Riceys comes from:","The Aube, in Champagne",["The Loire, near Anjou","Provence, near Aix","Burgundy, near Dijon"],"A rare still Pinot Noir rosé."]
]});

L.push({id:"s9", unit:"S1", title:"Classic Pairings and the Cheese Course", mins:9,
summary:"The pairings examiners treat as canon, why each one works, how to handle the difficult ingredients, and how to run a cheese course.",
body:`
<p>The pairing principles tell you why a match works. The classics are the matches so well proven that examiners and guests expect you to know them by heart. Learn each with its reason, because the Court's follow-up question is always "why?"</p>
<h3>The canon</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Dish</th><th>Classic wine</th><th>Why it works</th></tr>
<tr><td>Oysters</td><td>Chablis, Muscadet sur lie, Champagne</td><td>High acid and saline, lean body; no oak to fight the brine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caviar</td><td>Champagne (Blanc de Blancs)</td><td>Bubbles and acid cut the fat and salt</td></tr>
<tr><td>Foie gras</td><td>Sauternes; Alsace Vendange Tardive; Tokaji</td><td>Sweetness and acid against rich, fatty liver</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh goat cheese (Crottin de Chavignol)</td><td>Sancerre</td><td>Acid meets acid; the regional match</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roquefort</td><td>Sauternes</td><td>Sweet against salt</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stilton</td><td>Vintage Port</td><td>Sweet, strong wine against salty blue</td></tr>
<tr><td>Comté</td><td>Vin Jaune</td><td>Nut meets nut; both from the Jura</td></tr>
<tr><td>Munster</td><td>Alsace Gewurztraminer</td><td>Aromatic power to match a pungent washed rind</td></tr>
<tr><td>Jamón ibérico, almonds, olives</td><td>Fino or Manzanilla</td><td>Salt and tang; flor wines love cured fat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sole meunière; lobster with butter</td><td>Chablis Premier Cru; Meursault</td><td>Weight for weight: butter with a rich, oaked white</td></tr>
<tr><td>Choucroute garnie</td><td>Alsace Riesling</td><td>Acid for pork fat and sauerkraut</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coq au vin, bœuf bourguignon</td><td>Red Burgundy</td><td>The wine in the pot is the wine in the glass</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roast lamb</td><td>Pauillac; Rioja Reserva</td><td>Tannin binds to protein and fat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bistecca alla fiorentina</td><td>Chianti Classico, Brunello</td><td>High acid and tannin against charred beef and olive oil</td></tr>
<tr><td>White truffle pasta or risotto</td><td>Barolo, Barbaresco</td><td>Earth to earth; both from Piedmont</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cassoulet, duck confit</td><td>Cahors, Madiran</td><td>Dense tannin for very rich meat</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tomato-sauced pasta, pizza</td><td>Sangiovese, Barbera</td><td>Acidic food needs a wine at least as acidic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thai or Sichuan heat</td><td>Off-dry Riesling Kabinett or Spätlese</td><td>Sugar soothes chili; low alcohol doesn't burn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sushi and sashimi</td><td>Junmai sake; Champagne; dry Riesling</td><td>Delicate, no tannin; sake echoes umami</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dark chocolate</td><td>Banyuls, Maury, Ruby or LBV Port</td><td>The wine must be sweeter than the dessert</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tarte Tatin, crème brûlée</td><td>Coteaux du Layon, Sauternes, Tokaji Aszú</td><td>Sweet with sweet; acid keeps it fresh</td></tr></table>
<h3>The difficult ingredients</h3>
<ul><li><b>Artichoke</b> (cynarin makes wine taste oddly sweet) and <b>asparagus</b>: unoaked, herbal whites such as Grüner Veltliner or Sauvignon Blanc.</li>
<li><b>Eggs</b> coat the palate: sparkling wine.</li>
<li><b>Vinaigrette</b>: the wine must be sharper than the dressing.</li>
<li><b>Chili heat</b> magnifies alcohol and tannin: go low in both, with some sweetness.</li>
<li><b>Umami</b> (soy, mushrooms, aged cheese) makes tannic reds taste bitter; salt and acid in the dish repair it.</li>
<li><b>Very sweet desserts</b> flatten any wine that is less sweet.</li></ul>
<h3>Cheese by family</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Family</th><th>Examples</th><th>What to pour</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh and goat</td><td>Chèvre, Crottin, feta</td><td>Sancerre, Pouilly-Fumé, dry rosé</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bloomy rind</td><td>Brie, Camembert, Chaource</td><td>Champagne, Chablis, cider</td></tr>
<tr><td>Washed rind</td><td>Époisses, Munster, Taleggio</td><td>Gewurztraminer, Pinot Gris, off-dry Riesling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alpine and cooked</td><td>Comté, Gruyère, Beaufort</td><td>Vin Jaune, Savagnin, aged Chardonnay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard, aged</td><td>Parmigiano Reggiano, Manchego, aged Gouda</td><td>Amarone, Rioja Reserva, Oloroso, Tawny Port</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blue</td><td>Roquefort, Stilton, Gorgonzola</td><td>Sauternes, Vintage Port, Recioto, Tokaji</td></tr></table>
<p>The uncomfortable truth for red-wine drinkers: <b>white and sweet wines suit most cheese better than big reds</b>. Salt and creamy fat make firm tannin taste metallic and bitter. If a table insists on red with a mixed board, choose a soft, mature one.</p>
<h3>Running the course</h3>
<ul><li>Serve cheese at room temperature: out of the refrigerator about an hour ahead.</li>
<li>Plate and describe from mildest to strongest, and tell the guest the order.</li>
<li>Plan about 1 to 1.5 oz per cheese, three to five cheeses.</li>
<li>One wine for a board: an off-dry Riesling, a Tawny Port or a mature white Burgundy covers the most ground.</li></ul>
<h3>Ordering wines through a meal</h3>
<p>Light before heavy, dry before sweet, white before red, simple before complex. Break a rule only on purpose: Sauternes with foie gras at the start works because the next course resets the palate.</p>`,
floor:`<p>In the Certified practical you are given a dish and must name a wine, a producer and a reason in one breath: "With the lamb I'd suggest the 2016 Château Pontet-Canet from Pauillac; its tannin will soften against the meat." Rehearse ten of these aloud.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET marks the mechanism, not the tradition: sweetness in food raises bitterness and acidity in wine; salt and acid in food soften them. Use the classics as your examples, then explain them in those terms.</p>`,
terms:[["Classic pairing","A match so established that examiners expect it: Sancerre and goat cheese, Sauternes and Roquefort."],["Cynarin","Compound in artichoke that makes wine taste sweeter and stranger."],["Washed rind","Cheese rinsed in brine or spirit as it ripens: orange, sticky and pungent."],["Bloomy rind","Cheese with a soft white mould rind, such as Brie."],["Regional pairing","Matching a dish with the wine of its home region."]],
quiz:[
[1,"The classic wine for Roquefort is:","Sauternes",["Chablis","Barolo","Fino Sherry"],"Sweet against salt. Stilton's classic partner is Vintage Port."],
[1,"Crottin de Chavignol is traditionally served with:","Sancerre",["Meursault","Rioja Reserva","Amarone"],"Goat cheese and Sauvignon Blanc from the same hills: acid meets acid."],
[2,"Comté's classic regional partner is:","Vin Jaune",["Vouvray","Condrieu","Muscadet"],"Both come from the Jura, and both taste of walnut."],
[2,"Why does off-dry Riesling suit spicy food?","Sugar soothes heat and alcohol is low",["Tannin binds the chili oil","Oak adds sweetness","High alcohol numbs the palate"],"Chili magnifies alcohol and tannin, so avoid both."],
[2,"Artichokes are difficult with wine because of:","Cynarin",["Tannin","Capsaicin","Lactic acid"],"Cynarin makes the next sip taste oddly sweet. Choose a crisp, unoaked white."],
[2,"A dessert wine should be:","Sweeter than the dessert",["Drier than the dessert","Higher in tannin than the dessert","Colder than the dessert"],"A sweeter dish makes the wine taste thin, sour and bitter."],
[2,"Which wines suit most cheeses best?","White and sweet wines",["Young tannic reds","Heavily oaked reds","High-alcohol reds"],"Salt and creamy fat make firm tannin taste metallic and bitter."],
[2,"A cheese board should be eaten in what order?","Mildest to strongest",["Strongest to mildest","Hardest to softest","Oldest to youngest"],"A strong blue first would flatten everything after it."],
[3,"Munster's classic Alsace partner is:","Gewurztraminer",["Sylvaner","Crémant d'Alsace","Pinot Blanc"],"An aromatic, often off-dry wine with the power to meet a pungent washed rind."],
[2,"White truffle dishes classically call for:","Barolo or Barbaresco",["Amarone","Prosecco Superiore","Brunello di Montalcino"],"A Piedmont pairing: Nebbiolo's earth and perfume echo the truffle."]
]});

L.push({id:"b9", unit:"S2", title:"Deep Dive: Cocktail Families and Bar Service", mins:9,
summary:"Cocktails by family, so one template unlocks ten drinks; method, glass and garnish; and the service language guests use.",
body:`
<p>The Court tests cocktails from Certified upward, and the Master service exam treats them like wine: spec, method, glass, garnish and a line of history. Memorizing forty recipes one by one fails under pressure. Learn six families and the classics fall into place. Specs below are one common version of each; houses differ by a quarter-ounce.</p>
<h3>1. Spirit-forward, stirred</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Drink</th><th>Build</th></tr>
<tr><td>Old Fashioned</td><td>Bourbon or rye, sugar, Angostura bitters; rocks, orange twist</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sazerac</td><td>Rye (or Cognac), sugar, Peychaud's bitters, absinthe rinse; no ice, lemon twist. New Orleans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manhattan</td><td>2 rye, 1 sweet vermouth, Angostura; up, cherry. With Scotch it is a <b>Rob Roy</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Martini</td><td>Gin and dry vermouth; up, olive or twist. With an onion it is a <b>Gibson</b>. The <b>Vesper</b> adds vodka and Lillet</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negroni</td><td>Equal gin, Campari, sweet vermouth; rocks, orange. Bourbon instead of gin: <b>Boulevardier</b>. Prosecco instead of gin: <b>Negroni Sbagliato</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Vieux Carré</td><td>Rye, Cognac, sweet vermouth, Bénédictine, Peychaud's and Angostura. New Orleans</td></tr></table>
<h3>2. Sours: spirit, citrus, sweetener</h3>
<p>The template is about 2 parts spirit, 1 part citrus, ¾ part sweetener, shaken hard.</p>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Drink</th><th>Build</th></tr>
<tr><td>Daiquiri</td><td>White rum, lime, sugar; up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Whiskey Sour</td><td>Bourbon, lemon, sugar, optional egg white</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gimlet</td><td>Gin, lime, sugar (classically lime cordial)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pisco Sour</td><td>Pisco, lime, sugar, egg white, drops of Angostura on the foam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caipirinha</td><td>Cachaça, muddled lime, sugar; built on ice. Brazil</td></tr>
<tr><td>Margarita</td><td>Tequila, lime, orange liqueur; salt rim optional</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sidecar</td><td>Cognac, orange liqueur, lemon; sugar rim optional</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cosmopolitan</td><td>Citron vodka, orange liqueur, lime, a little cranberry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Last Word</td><td>Equal gin, green Chartreuse, maraschino, lime</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corpse Reviver No. 2</td><td>Equal gin, Lillet, orange liqueur, lemon; absinthe rinse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Aviation</td><td>Gin, maraschino, crème de violette, lemon</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mai Tai</td><td>Aged rum, lime, orange curaçao, orgeat (almond). Trader Vic, 1944</td></tr></table>
<p>The Margarita, Sidecar and Cosmopolitan are sours sweetened with orange liqueur: the same drink with a different base spirit.</p>
<h3>3. Long drinks: a sour or a spirit, lengthened</h3>
<p><b>Tom Collins</b> (gin sour plus soda), <b>Mojito</b> (rum, lime, sugar, mint, soda), <b>Paloma</b> (tequila, lime, grapefruit soda), <b>Moscow Mule</b> (vodka, lime, ginger beer, copper mug), <b>Dark 'n' Stormy</b> (dark rum and ginger beer), <b>Gin and Tonic</b>, <b>Mint Julep</b> (bourbon, mint, sugar, crushed ice in a metal cup; the Kentucky Derby drink).</p>
<h3>4. Wine and bubbles</h3>
<p><b>French 75</b> (gin, lemon, sugar, topped with Champagne), <b>Champagne Cocktail</b> (sugar cube soaked in Angostura, Champagne), <b>Kir</b> and <b>Kir Royale</b> (crème de cassis with Aligoté or Champagne), <b>Bellini</b> (white peach purée and Prosecco; Harry's Bar, Venice), <b>Mimosa</b> (orange juice and sparkling wine), <b>Aperol Spritz</b> (3 Prosecco, 2 Aperol, 1 soda), <b>Americano</b> (Campari, sweet vermouth, soda).</p>
<h3>5. Coffee, cream and savory</h3>
<p><b>Espresso Martini</b> (vodka, coffee liqueur, fresh espresso), <b>Irish Coffee</b> (Irish whiskey, hot coffee, sugar, floated cream), <b>Bloody Mary</b> (vodka, tomato juice, lemon, Worcestershire, hot sauce, celery salt).</p>
<h3>Method, glass, garnish</h3>
<ul><li><b>Shake</b> anything with citrus, juice, cream or egg: ice breaks it up and aerates it. With egg white, shake once without ice first (a dry shake) for foam.</li>
<li><b>Stir</b> all-spirit drinks for about 30 seconds: cold, clear and silky. Shaking a Manhattan clouds it.</li>
<li><b>Build</b> long drinks in the glass. <b>Muddle</b> gently: bruise mint, don't shred it.</li>
<li>Melted ice is an ingredient: a finished cocktail is roughly a fifth to a quarter water.</li>
<li><b>Glasses:</b> coupe or Nick & Nora for drinks served up; rocks glass; Collins or highball; flute; julep cup; copper mug.</li>
<li>A citrus <b>twist</b> is expressed over the drink so its oils land on the surface.</li></ul>
<h3>The words guests use</h3>
<p><b>Neat</b>: straight from the bottle, room temperature, no ice. <b>Up</b>: chilled with ice, then strained into a stemmed glass. <b>On the rocks</b>: over ice. <b>Dry</b> Martini: less vermouth. <b>Dirty</b>: with olive brine. <b>With a twist</b>: citrus peel. Always confirm the spirit and brand, the garnish, and up or rocks before you leave the table.</p>
<p>A US standard drink holds about 14 g of pure alcohol: 1.5 oz of 80-proof spirit, 5 oz of 12% wine or 12 oz of 5% beer. Many classic cocktails hold two.</p>`,
floor:`<p>At the table: "Gin or vodka? Up or on the rocks? Olive or a twist?" Three questions, asked before the guest has to volunteer them, is the difference between a server and a sommelier. Know which drinks are stirred; examiners ask.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>WSET's wine qualifications don't examine cocktails; its spirits qualifications do, by family and balance (strong, weak, sour, sweet). The family method here is the same logic.</p>`,
terms:[["Sour","Cocktail family of spirit, citrus and sweetener, shaken."],["Dry shake","Shaking without ice first to foam egg white."],["Up","Chilled with ice and strained into a stemmed glass, served without ice."],["Neat","Poured straight from the bottle at room temperature, no ice."],["Orgeat","Almond syrup, essential to the Mai Tai."],["Peychaud's","New Orleans bitters used in the Sazerac and Vieux Carré."],["Standard drink","About 14 g of pure alcohol in the US: 1.5 oz spirit, 5 oz wine or 12 oz beer."]],
quiz:[
[1,"Which of these drinks should be stirred, not shaken?","Manhattan",["Daiquiri","Margarita","Whiskey Sour"],"All-spirit drinks are stirred to stay clear. Drinks with citrus are shaken."],
[2,"A Boulevardier is a Negroni made with:","Bourbon in place of gin",["Prosecco in place of gin","Aperol in place of Campari","Mezcal in place of vermouth"],"Prosecco in place of gin makes a Negroni Sbagliato."],
[2,"The basic sour template is spirit, citrus and:","A sweetener",["Bitters","Vermouth","Soda water"],"About 2 parts spirit, 1 citrus, ¾ sweetener, shaken."],
[2,"A Last Word is equal parts gin, lime, maraschino and:","Green Chartreuse",["Campari","Dry vermouth","Crème de violette"],"Crème de violette belongs to the Aviation."],
[2,"A Manhattan made with Scotch is called a:","Rob Roy",["Gibson","Vesper","Sidecar"],"A Gibson is a Martini with an onion; a Vesper adds vodka and Lillet."],
[2,"Orgeat, an almond syrup, is essential to the:","Mai Tai",["Mojito","Paloma","Pisco Sour"],"Created by Trader Vic in 1944: aged rum, lime, orange curaçao and orgeat."],
[2,"A guest orders a whiskey 'neat.' You serve it:","At room temperature with no ice",["Chilled and strained into a coupe","Over one large ice cube","With a splash of soda"],"Chilled and strained is 'up'; over ice is 'on the rocks.'"],
[3,"The Vieux Carré and the Sazerac both come from:","New Orleans",["New York","London","Havana"],"Both use Peychaud's bitters, also from New Orleans."],
[2,"Why dry-shake a Pisco Sour before adding ice?","To foam the egg white",["To chill the glass","To dissolve the bitters","To reduce dilution"],"Shaking without ice whips the egg white; a second shake with ice chills and dilutes."],
[1,"A US standard drink contains about:","14 g of pure alcohol",["5 g of pure alcohol","30 g of pure alcohol","50 g of pure alcohol"],"Equal to 1.5 oz of 80-proof spirit, 5 oz of 12% wine or 12 oz of 5% beer."]
]});

L.push({id:"h4", unit:"H", title:"The Vintage Chart, Region by Region", mins:10,
summary:"The years to know cold for Advanced theory and the dining room: legends, benchmarks and the difficult vintages, with 2017 to 2024 year by year.",
body:`
<p>The Advanced theory paper asks vintages directly, and the Master exam asks why. This chart extends the first vintages lesson into the full working memory of a senior sommelier. It records broad critical consensus: argue at the edges, and remember that a great grower in a hard year often beats an average one in a famous year.</p>
<h3>France</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Benchmarks</th><th>Difficult or atypical</th></tr>
<tr><td>Bordeaux (red)</td><td>1945, 1947, 1959, 1961, <b>1982</b>, 1989, 1990, 2000, 2005, 2009, 2010, 2015, 2016, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2022</td><td>1991 (frost), 1992, 2007, 2013 (rain, rot), 2017 (frost), 2021 (cool), 2024 (wet, small)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sauternes</td><td>1988, 1989, 1990, <b>2001</b>, 2007, 2009, 2011, 2015</td><td>2012: Château d'Yquem made no grand vin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Burgundy (red)</td><td>1978, 1985, 1990, 1999, 2002, 2005, 2009, 2010, 2015, 2019, 2020, 2022</td><td>2004 (green notes), 2011, 2021 (frost, tiny crop)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Burgundy (white)</td><td>2002, 2010, <b>2014</b>, 2017, 2020, 2022</td><td>2003 (heat); bottles from about 1995 to 2005 are at risk of premature oxidation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Champagne</td><td>1988, 1990, 1996 (very high acid), 2002, 2004, <b>2008</b>, 2012, 2013</td><td>2001, 2003 (heat), 2017 (rot)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Northern Rhône</td><td>1978, 1990, 1991, 1999, 2009, 2010, 2015, 2016, 2017, 2019, 2020</td><td>2002, 2008, 2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Southern Rhône</td><td>1978, 1989, 1990, 1998, 2001, 2007, 2010, 2016, 2019</td><td>2002 (floods), 2008</td></tr></table>
<h3>Italy, Spain, Portugal, Germany</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Region</th><th>Benchmarks</th><th>Difficult or atypical</th></tr>
<tr><td>Barolo and Barbaresco</td><td>1978, 1982, 1989, 1990, 1996, 1999, 2001, 2004, 2006, <b>2010</b>, 2013, <b>2016</b>, 2019</td><td>2002 (hail, rain), 2003 (heat), 2014 (rain; Barbaresco fared better)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tuscany (Brunello, Chianti Classico)</td><td>1985, 1990, 1997, 1999, 2001, 2004, 2006, 2010, 2015, 2016, 2019</td><td>2002, 2014</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rioja</td><td>1964, 1982, 1994, 1995, 2001, 2004, 2005, 2010</td><td>2013</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vintage Port (general declarations)</td><td>1945, 1963, 1966, 1970, 1977, 1983, 1985, 1994, 1997, 2000, 2003, 2007, 2011, <b>2016 and 2017</b> (a rare back-to-back pair)</td><td>Other years go to single-quinta Vintage, LBV or Colheita</td></tr>
<tr><td>Germany (Riesling)</td><td>1971, 1990, 2001, 2005, 2015, 2019, 2021 (classic, high acid)</td><td>2003 (heat), 2010 (extreme acidity, small crop)</td></tr></table>
<h3>United States</h3>
<p><b>Napa Cabernet:</b> 1974, 1985, 1991, 1994, 1997, 2001, 2002, 2007, 2012, 2013, 2016, 2018, 2019, 2021. Difficult: 1998 and 2011 (cool, wet), 2017 (October fires, after most of the harvest), 2020 (smoke; many reds not released). California, Australia, Argentina and Chile vary less than Europe because sunshine is more reliable, but the gap is narrowing as heat and fire seasons grow.</p>
<h3>2017 to 2024 in Europe, year by year</h3>
<table class="t"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
<tr><td>2017</td><td>April frost across France cut crops in Bordeaux, Chablis and Champagne; hot and dry in the Rhône and Piedmont. Port declared.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2018</td><td>Hot and generous: ripe, large crops in Burgundy and Champagne; mildew troubled Bordeaux in spring, then a superb summer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2019</td><td>Heat spikes (France passed 45°C in June) yet fresh, concentrated wines. Excellent almost everywhere.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2020</td><td>Early, dry and ripe. Completes the 2018-2019-2020 trio in Bordeaux.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2021</td><td>Severe April frost, then mildew. Small, cool, lighter wines in France; high-acid, classic Riesling in Germany.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2022</td><td>Record heat and drought; forest fires near Bordeaux. Wines proved fresher and better balanced than feared.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2023</td><td>Large crops in Burgundy and Champagne; heavy mildew in Bordeaux; late-summer heat.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024</td><td>A wet year in much of France, with mildew and one of the smallest harvests in decades. Lighter styles.</td></tr></table>
<h3>How to use a chart</h3>
<ul><li><b>On the floor:</b> a guest ordering a 2013 Bordeaux deserves to hear that it is a lighter year to drink now; a guest ordering 2016 Barolo should hear that it wants time or a decanter.</li>
<li><b>Buying:</b> the best values are good producers in unfashionable years, and the second and third labels of great years.</li>
<li><b>Southern Hemisphere:</b> harvest runs from about February to April, so a 2024 from Australia is half a year older than a 2024 from France.</li>
<li><b>Non-vintage:</b> Champagne, Sherry, Tawny Port and most Madeira are blended across years so that the chart doesn't matter.</li></ul>`,
floor:`<p>For Advanced theory, drill this as ordering and matching: "Arrange these Port declarations from oldest to youngest," "Which was the frost year?" For the dining room, know one sentence about each of the last ten vintages on your own list.</p>`,
analyst:`<p>Diploma and MW answers use vintages as evidence: "The 2021 frost and the heat of 2022 illustrate the two faces of climate risk in Burgundy." One accurate vintage example is worth more than a general statement.</p>`,
terms:[["General declaration","A year in which most Port shippers declare a vintage."],["Premature oxidation","'Premox': white Burgundies of about 1995 to 2005 that aged far too fast."],["Off vintage","A less-regarded year, often good value from top growers."],["Back-to-back declaration","Two consecutive declared Port vintages, such as 2016 and 2017."]],
quiz:[
[2,"Which Bordeaux vintage made Robert Parker's reputation?","1982",["1961","1990","2000"],"Parker praised the ripe 1982s when other critics doubted them."],
[2,"Which year is the modern benchmark for vintage Champagne?","2008",["2003","2011","2017"],"High acid and ripeness together. 2003 was a heat year and 2017 was hit by rot."],
[3,"In which year did Château d'Yquem make no grand vin?","2012",["2001","2009","2015"],"2001 is the legendary Sauternes vintage; 2012 was rejected."],
[2,"Which pair are both top Barolo vintages?","2010 and 2016",["2002 and 2014","2003 and 2017","2011 and 2012"],"2002 and 2014 were wet and difficult."],
[3,"Which of these years was a general declaration for Vintage Port?","1977",["1978","1981","1993"],"The classic run: 1963, 1966, 1970, 1977, 1983, 1985, 1994, 1997, 2000, 2003, 2007, 2011, 2016, 2017."],
[2,"Europe's 2021 vintage is remembered for:","Spring frost and a small crop",["Record heat and drought","Wildfire smoke","A very large, ripe crop"],"Frost in April, then mildew. Record heat and drought describes 2022."],
[2,"White Burgundy from about 1995 to 2005 carries a risk of:","Premature oxidation",["Cork taint in every bottle","Refermentation","Smoke taint"],"Known as premox: wines browned and tired years early."],
[2,"A 2024 wine from Australia was harvested:","About six months before a 2024 from France",["At the same time as a 2024 from France","About six months after a 2024 from France","A full year before a 2024 from France"],"Southern Hemisphere harvest runs from about February to April."],
[3,"Which was a difficult Bordeaux vintage?","2013",["2010","2016","2019"],"A cool, wet year with rot. The others are among the greatest."]
]});
